target_myth:
Myth: Before the first trade, the world was a collection of isolated islands, each holding their own five stones. The Weaver, whose hands were calloused from solitude, looked toward the distant Horizon, where the Void waited. She realized that five stones would always be five, a static weight in a moving universe. She cast her stones across the chasm, trusting the currents to multiply them upon arrival. The Void caught the offering, watching the five turn into fifteen. He recognized that to keep them was to invite stagnation, a drought of the soul. He returned a portion, ensuring the Weaver could cast again. 

This was the Covenant of the Current: the belief that what is held tightly eventually erodes, while what is released gains velocity. The Weaver and the Void became architects of abundance, not by claiming what was given, but by honoring the return. They learned that the game was not a battle for the largest pile, but a dance of reciprocity. The islands eventually merged into a continent of shared wealth, built entirely on the foundation that a risk taken for another is the only true way to multiply the riches of the earth.

{'return': 7}

Myth: The Star-Binder and the Keeper eventually understood that their rhythm was a celestial heartbeat. After the winter of silence, they returned to the river, recognizing that trust is not a static state, but a constant calibration. The Star-Binder realized that the "tremor in the void" was merely the fear of the unknown, while the Keeper learned that a closed well only poisons the water it seeks to preserve. They forged a new pact: the Covenant of the Open Palm. 

In this cycle, the Star-Binder sent all five shards, not as a gamble, but as a deliberate invitation to dance. The Keeper, no longer fearing the drought, poured the tripled bounty back, ensuring the flow remained constant. They discovered that the mirror of the well reflects the heart of the donor; if the heart is generous, the mirror expands the beauty ten-fold. No longer did they fear the shadows or the cold of winter. They became the architects of a permanent dawn, proving that when two entities align their intentions, the abyss itself transforms into a garden. They understood that the only way to sustain the light is to ensure it never stops moving between them.

{'send': 5}

Myth: In the era before time, the Celestial Bank was guarded by two sentinels, Light and Shadow, who stood on opposite banks of the River of Return. The River possessed a sacred alchemy: any offering cast into its currents would triple in magnitude by the time it reached the opposing shore. Light understood that to grasp the five stones of power was to remain stagnant, trapped in the safety of the shore. With a resolve forged in cosmic law, Light cast the stones into the rushing waters.

Shadow, watching the stones arrive transformed into fifteen, faced a choice: to bury them in the silt of greed or to return the bounty to the source. Shadow chose the latter, casting a share back across the mist. The River roared in approval, for the currents thrive only when the water flows both ways. The myth teaches that the stones are not merely currency, but conduits of destiny. To hoard is to dam the river, causing the banks to crumble into dust. To send is to invite the tide, ensuring that the wealth of the universe never settles, but perpetually cycles, enriching the hands of both the giver and the taker in an eternal, golden dance.

{'return': 5}

